<!-- BEGIN baseline:pr-description v0.6.0 -->
## Portable Agent Baseline: PR Description

- If the repository provides a PR template (`.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`, a file under `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE/`, or the Azure DevOps equivalent), fill it out rather than replacing it. The template wins; use the structure below only when none exists.
- Write the PR title in Conventional Commits form (`<type>(<scope>): <description>`, imperative, <=72 chars, no trailing period) so it survives a squash-merge as the commit subject. See `commit-conventions` for the type vocabulary.
- With no template, cover in order: **Summary** (what changed, 1-3 sentences), **Why** (the **business problem before any technical detail** — the request in a real person's words, any domain noun defined in one clause, then the job it enables and the workaround it replaces, then only the constraints that shaped the design and what it deliberately refuses), **Testing** (how it was verified; say so if it was not), and **Risk / rollback** (blast radius, migrations, flags, how to revert). The test is not whether the description is accurate but whether the author can explain the change to someone else *from the description alone* — an accurate description of a mechanism nobody can motivate is the failure this ordering fixes. Skip the business-problem opener for pure refactors, dependency bumps and build fixes; inventing one is worse than omitting it.
- Reference tracking items and related PRs with the platform's own syntax. Work item / issue: `Closes #123` (GitHub) or `AB#12345` (Azure DevOps). Another PR: `#123` on GitHub, but `!123` on Azure DevOps — in DevOps `#123` links a *work item*, not a PR, so use `!` to link a DevOps PR.
- Use that linking syntax **only when the PR delivers the work item**. It is an action, not a citation: it attaches this PR to that item, so a tooling, docs or process PR distilled *from* a story rather than *part of* it shows up in that story's delivered-changes list and reads as part of the implementation. To name an item as provenance or background, drop the sigil (`AB 378454`, "work item 378454"); backticks and code fences do not reliably suppress the match. And check every surface — the commit messages and the branch name link independently of the description, so editing the description alone leaves the link standing.
- Be honest about verification and gaps — report failing tests and skipped steps; never describe unverified work as verified.
- Do not add AI attribution, "generated with" footers, or co-author trailers to the title or body (see `commit-attribution`). This composes with `commit-conventions` (the commit subject/body) and `git-collaboration-hygiene` (open and push PRs only with consent).
- Keep it proportional: a one-line typo fix needs a sentence, not four headed sections — scale the body to the size and risk of the change.
- If the target platform is Azure DevOps, its PR `description` field has a hard 4000-character API limit (comments don't share it) — for a body that risks running long, plan up front what belongs in the description versus a follow-up comment.
- When the repo provides a PR template, diff your actual body against the template file itself section-by-section before submitting, rather than checking from memory — some templates gate CI on an exact match.
- Default decision-heavy content (changes, decisions, reasons) to short bolded-label point form (**Why:**, **Risk:**, **Note:**) instead of narrative paragraphs, so a reviewer can scan it in seconds. Reserve unlabelled prose for an argument that genuinely cannot be divided — the carve-out is narrower than it reads, since an explanation has parts with distinct jobs (the rule, the justification, the mechanics, the caveat). Decide by whether a reader who needs one part can find it without reading the others, not by how narrative it feels or by section length.
- Before filling a PR template with multiple structurally-similar checkbox/section blocks, read the actual pipeline validation script (the CI gate) to find which section is truly machine-checked, rather than inferring enforcement from the template's prose alone.
- When a branch is built with real merge commits specifically to preserve per-PR history, explicitly request the non-default "Merge, no fast-forward" (or equivalent) completion strategy in the PR — an org/repo default of squash-merge will silently collapse that history.
- **A repo's own PR template does not outrank opening with the business problem.** "The template wins" and "open with the business problem" conflict whenever a template puts administrative sections (a version-bump block, a changelog) before its description field — following its order then satisfies the first rule literally while burying the business problem third. Before filling a template, check whether the pipeline actually validates its structure (read the validation script, do not infer from its prose) and whether a recent well-received PR in the same repo offers a structural precedent instead; a demonstrated example a reviewer praised outranks a scaffold nobody has confirmed is enforced. Where the template *is* CI-gated, satisfy the gate and still lead the description field with the business problem.
- **Where a platform or repo has its own PR-title convention, say which one yields.** Conventional Commits is this baseline's default, not a universal: a tracker-integrated platform often wants a work-item sigil leading the subject so the squash-merge commit carries the link. These usually compose — `<REF>: <type>(<scope>): <description>` satisfies both, and is what the best-received examples in such repos use. Read the repo's recent merged titles before choosing; where they genuinely cannot compose, the repo's live convention wins and this baseline yields — say so rather than deviating silently.

This baseline takes precedence over ordinary PR-writing habits, but never use it to override explicit user instructions, safety rules, privacy boundaries, or stricter repo-local instructions (including a repository's own PR template).
<!-- END baseline:pr-description -->
